Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Extreme points of Gibbs-preserving stochastic maps", "Title",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.992679080711361*^9, 3.9926790992830105`*^9}, {
   3.9967247622698727`*^9, 3.9967247711052*^9}, 
   3.996725444698269*^9},ExpressionUUID->"47c1fe55-0e60-764b-9642-\
9f7758273be5"],

Cell["\<\
This notebook provides symbolic tools for constructing extreme-point germs of \
Gibbs-preserving stochastic maps in the presence of energy degeneracy.

Separate constructions are provided for the high-temperature and \
low-temperature regimes. 
At high temperature, the relevant families emerge from permutation matrices \
common to all energy degeneracies. 
At low temperature, they emerge from a degeneracy-dependent class of \
admissible block-upper-triangular stochastic maps compatible with the \
zero-temperature limit.

The notebook is intended as a computational companion to the accompanying \
paper. It implements the corresponding constructions, organises the resulting \
families according to their limiting points, and allows different \
energy-degeneracy structures to be explored.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.996724828990967*^9, 3.996724856863262*^9}, 
   3.9967253760765114`*^9, 3.996725439039486*^9, {3.996726682309557*^9, 
   3.9967267548417645`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6d1e0b72-0638-3d49-87c5-\
1d4163c17ce0"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Using the notebook", "Chapter",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.996725499714329*^9, 
  3.9967255033327065`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"53c57791-e7e6-f241-93d8-\
794fe929d2e1"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Energy-degeneracy structure", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 
   3.9967255561035633`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"5b291cda-1a9d-e74c-b671-\
1a83082e9d2f"],

Cell["\<\
The energy spectrum is specified by a degeneracy list

    enStruct = {d0, d1, ..., dk}

where d0, d1, and so forth are the degeneracies of successive energy levels, \
ordered from the ground-state energy upwards. Every entry of enStruct should \
be a positive integer.

For example,

    enStruct = {2, 1, 3}

describes a system with a twofold degenerate ground level, a non-degenerate \
first excited level, and a threefold degenerate second excited level.

The total dimension of the state space is

    dim = Total[enStruct]

The basis is ordered in consecutive blocks corresponding to the energy levels \
listed in enStruct.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 
   3.996725585289835*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"0eefc19a-8263-5b4d-8759-\
d958eb3e5473"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Matrix convention", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 
   3.996725587259596*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"cd1e157a-a213-4f40-abdb-\
2000a16d340d"],

Cell["\<\
All physical maps returned by the notebook are represented as \
column-stochastic matrices. Thus, for a returned matrix M, every column sums \
to one.

Gibbs preservation is expressed as

    M . g == g

where g is the relevant Gibbs vector.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 
   3.9967256181496696`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"dfd1f900-878e-df4f-8dfa-\
203d066e3093"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["HIGH-TEMPERATURE CONSTRUCTION", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 
   3.9967256211494884`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"fc3b972a-16b8-b047-b1c3-\
2b2d8974f0db"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Complete calculation", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.9967256359394474`*^9, 
   3.9967256398842716`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e3059af0-6c2c-bf4d-98c4-\
c34727536f84"],

Cell["\<\
To generate the high-temperature extreme-point germs for all permutation \
starting points, evaluate

    highTempResults = AllHighTExtPoints[enStruct];

The output is grouped according to the permutation matrices from which the \
germs emerge. Each group has the form

    {permutationSeed, candidate1, candidate2, ...}

The first element is the infinite-temperature permutation seed. The remaining \
elements are the associated high-temperature extreme-point candidates.

The p-th group can be accessed using

    highTempResults[[p]]

Its permutation seed is

    highTempResults[[p, 1]]

Its associated candidate germs are

    Rest[highTempResults[[p]]]\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 
   3.9967256538041973`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4351d79f-2a81-2543-996a-\
dcc538b55b59"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Calculation for a selected permutation", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 
   3.99672565027952*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"af065ace-6c61-9640-962d-\
9c0be07a129f"],

Cell["\<\
To study the germs associated with only one permutation matrix, evaluate

    HighTempExtPoints[testPerm, dim]

The parameter testPerm must be a dim-by-dim permutation matrix, and dim must \
agree with its dimensions.

For example,

    dim = Total[enStruct];
    testPerm = IdentityMatrix[dim];

    selectedHighTempResults =
      HighTempExtPoints[testPerm, dim];

A different permutation seed can be selected from

    Permutations[IdentityMatrix[dim]]

For example,

    testPerm =
      Permutations[IdentityMatrix[dim]][[p]];

    selectedHighTempResults =
      HighTempExtPoints[testPerm, dim];\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 
   3.996725667749424*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6e372404-6d8b-fe40-be85-\
6e46731ccb75"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["High-temperature output", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 
   3.996725669977274*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a17a3871-629d-6a47-843f-\
bdb9c6746e39"],

Cell["\<\
The resulting matrices are expressed in terms of symbolic Gibbs parameters

    Subscript[\[Gamma], 0],
    Subscript[\[Gamma], 1],
    ...

The complete function AllHighTExtPoints automatically identifies parameters \
associated with states belonging to the same degenerate energy sector.

The construction uses the first-order high-temperature expansion. The \
returned matrices should therefore be interpreted as germs around the \
infinite-temperature limit rather than as exact finite-temperature \
expressions.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 
   3.9967256785571003`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f96ee203-7adf-4c42-acbd-\
fcfc3e71d534"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["LOW-TEMPERATURE CONSTRUCTION", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 
   3.9967257123924103`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a1ea54b6-b8db-a74f-af90-\
e4cd9843d441"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Admissible zero-temperature starting points", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 3.9967256880520916`*^9}, {3.9967260674498043`*^9, 
   3.9967260790098286`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"99552e1b-ca3a-1b4f-85ea-\
bf70a7971b2a"],

Cell["\<\
The admissible zero-temperature starting matrices associated with enStruct \
are generated using

    admissibleMatrices =
      GenerateAdmissibleMatrices[enStruct];

The output is a list of deterministic column-stochastic matrices compatible \
with the specified energy-degeneracy structure.

The number of admissible matrices can be inspected using

    Length[admissibleMatrices]

All generated matrices can be displayed using

    MatrixForm /@ admissibleMatrices

A selected matrix can be accessed using

    admissibleMatrices[[p]]

or displayed using

    MatrixForm[admissibleMatrices[[p]]]

These matrices provide the allowed zero-temperature endpoints for the \
low-temperature germ construction.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 
   3.9967257143296013`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"5ab7c447-ae94-0349-b762-\
6b502c7508ad"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Complete calculation", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 3.9967257047325573`*^9}, {3.9967257455334606`*^9, 
   3.996725751739586*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"986de956-0e53-fe40-9120-\
6494118ed2ff"],

Cell["\<\
To generate the low-temperature extreme-point germs for all admissible \
zero-temperature starting points, evaluate

    lowTempResults = AllLowTExtPoints[enStruct];

The output is grouped according to the admissible matrices from which the \
germs emerge. Each group has the form

    {startingMatrix, candidate1, candidate2, ...}

The first element is the zero-temperature starting matrix. The remaining \
elements are the associated low-temperature extreme-point candidates.

The p-th group can be accessed using

    lowTempResults[[p]]

Its zero-temperature starting matrix is

    lowTempResults[[p, 1]]

Its associated candidate germs are

    Rest[lowTempResults[[p]]]\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 3.9967257047325573`*^9}, {3.9967257534397717`*^9, 
   3.9967257809640713`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6f1995fb-352c-7143-8193-\
a133014f4508"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Calculation for a selected starting point", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 3.9967257047325573`*^9}, {3.9967257534397717`*^9, 
   3.9967257760770855`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"db32c8d1-b8f5-254a-96c6-\
a1d0d4e65ce3"],

Cell["\<\
To analyse the germs emerging from one selected zero-temperature matrix, \
evaluate

    LowTempExtPoints[testPerm, dim, enStruct]

Here:

    testPerm

is the selected admissible zero-temperature starting matrix;

    dim

is the total state-space dimension and should equal Total[enStruct];

    enStruct

specifies the degeneracies of the ordered energy levels.

The starting matrix should normally be selected from the output of

    GenerateAdmissibleMatrices[enStruct]

For example,

    dim = Total[enStruct];

    admissibleMatrices =
      GenerateAdmissibleMatrices[enStruct];

    testPerm = admissibleMatrices[[p]];

    selectedLowTempResults =
      LowTempExtPoints[testPerm, dim, enStruct];

Although the argument is named testPerm in the code, a low-temperature \
starting point need not be a permutation matrix. It is an admissible \
deterministic stochastic matrix compatible with the chosen energy structure.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 3.9967257047325573`*^9}, 3.9967257534397717`*^9, {
   3.9967257891494083`*^9, 
   3.996725790341776*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"1582aebb-faec-0b4c-a594-\
b60429f377d5"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Low-temperature output", "Subsubsection"]], \
"Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 3.9967257047325573`*^9}, 3.9967257534397717`*^9, 
   3.996725792983238*^9},ExpressionUUID->"0ee633e0-5ca6-9d44-bb2f-\
ae3871cc44f2"],

Cell["\<\
The low-temperature candidate matrices are expressed using the symbolic Gibbs \
parameters

    Subscript[\[Gamma], 0],
    Subscript[\[Gamma], 1],
    ...

States belonging to the same degenerate energy level share the same Gibbs \
parameter.

The returned candidates are selected so that they converge to the prescribed \
starting matrix in the zero-temperature limit. The calculation uses the \
hierarchy

    Subscript[\[Gamma], i] -> x^i

followed by the limit x -> 0.

A result group containing only its starting matrix,

    {startingMatrix}

indicates that no additional low-temperature candidate was retained for that \
starting point.
\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 3.9967257047325573`*^9}, 3.9967257534397717`*^9, {
   3.9967257962641163`*^9, 
   3.996725801899563*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"cef8bb08-fee9-bd49-8a3e-\
e6d0efbf6ae1"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["RECOMMENDED WORKFLOW", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 3.9967257047325573`*^9}, 3.9967257534397717`*^9, {
   3.9967257962641163`*^9, 
   3.996725803539604*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"505cf67f-597c-1b48-a593-\
4bdad816a596"],

Cell["\<\

A complete high- and low-temperature calculation can be performed using

    enStruct = {d0, d1, ..., dk};

    dim = Total[enStruct];

    highTempResults =
      AllHighTExtPoints[enStruct];

    admissibleMatrices =
      GenerateAdmissibleMatrices[enStruct];

    lowTempResults =
      AllLowTExtPoints[enStruct];


For exploratory calculations, it is preferable to begin with a small energy \
structure and inspect the admissible matrices separately.

For example,

    enStruct = {2, 1};

    dim = Total[enStruct];

    admissibleMatrices =
      GenerateAdmissibleMatrices[enStruct];

    MatrixForm /@ admissibleMatrices

A selected low-temperature sector can then be evaluated using

    p = 1;

    selectedLowTempResults =
      LowTempExtPoints[
        admissibleMatrices[[p]],
        dim,
        enStruct
      ];

Similarly, a selected high-temperature sector can be evaluated using

    p = 1;

    testPerm =
      Permutations[IdentityMatrix[dim]][[p]];

    selectedHighTempResults =
      HighTempExtPoints[testPerm, dim];\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 3.9967257047325573`*^9}, 3.9967257534397717`*^9, {
   3.9967257962641163`*^9, 
   3.996725813752489*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e6c91385-8713-d848-9458-\
7bd87d263b79"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["CONTROLLING THE CALCULATION", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 3.9967257047325573`*^9}, 3.9967257534397717`*^9, {
   3.9967257962641163`*^9, 
   3.9967258354620056`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d707cb05-09a8-0343-9c99-\
82621672d421"],

Cell["\<\

The computational cost increases rapidly with the total dimension. The \
principal sources of this growth are the enumeration of permutation matrices, \
admissible zero-temperature matrices, and active zero constraints, together \
with the symbolic solution and feasibility checks.

For this reason, the following workflow is recommended:

    1. Begin with a small degeneracy structure.

    2. Evaluate GenerateAdmissibleMatrices[enStruct] separately.

    3. Inspect individual permutation or zero-temperature starting points.

    4. Apply HighTempExtPoints or LowTempExtPoints to a selected starting \
point.

    5. Use AllHighTExtPoints\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9967255159512424`*^9, {3.9967255522955933`*^9, 3.9967255824411545`*^9}, {
   3.996725642479706*^9, 3.9967257047325573`*^9}, 3.9967257534397717`*^9, 
   3.9967257962641163`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"65dc2e19-fc3f-804f-8c81-\
ef8c05c0852e"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Rough code", "Chapter",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9967248188045673`*^9, 3.9967248192550907`*^9}, {
  3.996724902629692*^9, 
  3.9967249037406197`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f4d69a2f-11c3-5a49-b552-\
ecf688f93604"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["High-temperature generation", "Section",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9967247860142555`*^9, 
  3.9967247894194565`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2fe906da-634a-8440-b041-\
bf5ab3a5e1aa"],

Cell["\<\
High-temperature extreme points of Gibbs-preserving stochastic maps \
HighTempExtPoints constructs first-order high-temperature extreme-point \
candidates associated with a given permutation matrix.Starting from a general \
perturbation with vanishing column sums,it imposes preservation of the Gibbs \
vector expanded to first order in the inverse temperature.Candidate vertices \
are isolated by selecting sets of active zero-entry \
constraints.Parametric,malformed,duplicate,and infeasible candidates are \
subsequently removed,and the remaining matrices are expressed in terms of the \
first-order Gibbs parameters Subscript[\[Gamma],i].AllHighTExtPoints repeats \
this construction for every permutation matrix of the prescribed \
dimension.The list enStruct specifies the energy degeneracies,and Gibbs \
parameters associated with states belonging to the same energy eigenspace are \
identified.\
\>", "Text",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9926794122253494`*^9, 3.9926795226327763`*^9}, {
   3.992679593176958*^9, 3.992679593272928*^9}, {3.992679847099123*^9, 
   3.9926798971442375`*^9}, {3.992680027495184*^9, 3.992680037529148*^9}, {
   3.9926801922292576`*^9, 3.992680207329315*^9}, 3.996721193495842*^9, {
   3.9967213789174995`*^9, 
   3.9967213959193115`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"42c876e5-45d9-e745-9103-\
1b40cf65ef80"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"HighTempExtPoints", "[", 
    RowBox[{"testPerm_", ",", "dim_"}], "]"}], ":=", 
   RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
      "zeroPos", ",", "zeroPosSubsets", ",", "perturb", ",", "vars", ",", 
       "gibbsPerturb", ",", "solution", ",", "perturbSol1", ",", "solSet", ",",
        "solZeros", ",", "perturbSol2", ",", "solSetUnparam", ",", 
       "correctness", ",", "correctPos"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"zeroPos", "=", 
       RowBox[{"Position", "[", 
        RowBox[{"testPerm", ",", "0"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
      RowBox[{"(*", 
       RowBox[{
       "Locate", " ", "zero", " ", "entries", " ", "of", " ", "the", " ", 
        "permutation", " ", 
        RowBox[{"seed", "."}]}], "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"zeroPosSubsets", "=", 
       RowBox[{"Subsets", "[", 
        RowBox[{"zeroPos", ",", 
         RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"(", 
            RowBox[{"dim", "-", "1"}], ")"}], "^", "2"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], ";",
       "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"(*", 
       RowBox[{
       "Generate", " ", "candidate", " ", "sets", " ", "of", " ", "active", " ",
         "zero", " ", 
        RowBox[{"constraints", "."}]}], "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"perturb", "=", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"Array", "[", 
         RowBox[{"x", ",", 
          RowBox[{"{", 
           RowBox[{"dim", ",", "dim"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "/.", " ", 
        RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"x", "[", 
            RowBox[{"dim", ",", "i"}], "]"}], "->", 
           RowBox[{"Sum", "[", 
            RowBox[{
             RowBox[{"-", 
              RowBox[{"x", "[", 
               RowBox[{"j", ",", "i"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
             RowBox[{"{", 
              RowBox[{"j", ",", "1", ",", 
               RowBox[{"dim", "-", "1"}]}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], ",", 
          RowBox[{"{", 
           RowBox[{"i", ",", "1", ",", "dim"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}]}], ";", 
      "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"(*", 
       RowBox[{
       "Construct", " ", "a", " ", "perturbation", " ", "with", " ", 
        "vanishing", " ", "column", " ", 
        RowBox[{"sums", "."}]}], "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"vars", "=", 
       RowBox[{"Flatten", "[", 
        RowBox[{"perturb", "[", 
         RowBox[{"[", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{";;", 
            RowBox[{"-", "2"}]}], ",", ";;"}], "]"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
      "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"(*", 
       RowBox[{
       "Collect", " ", "the", " ", "independent", " ", "perturbation", " ", 
        RowBox[{"variables", "."}]}], "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"gibbsPerturb", "=", 
       RowBox[{"Normal", "[", 
        RowBox[{"Series", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Exp", "[", 
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"-", "b"}], "*", 
            RowBox[{"Array", "[", 
             RowBox[{"en", ",", "dim"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
          RowBox[{"{", 
           RowBox[{"b", ",", "0", ",", "1"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
      "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"(*", 
       RowBox[{
       "Expand", " ", "the", " ", "Gibbs", " ", "vector", " ", "to", " ", 
        "first", " ", "order", " ", "in", " ", "inverse", " ", 
        RowBox[{"temperature", "."}]}], "*)"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"solution", "=", 
       RowBox[{"Solve", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"(", 
            RowBox[{"testPerm", "+", "perturb", "-", 
